//! Global scheduled automation.
//!
//! The schedule domain owns four durable facts and nothing else:
//!
//! - a **definition** says *when* and *what*: a workspace, a deterministic
//!   shell command, one trigger, a permission profile and an optional model;
//! - an **occurrence** is one slot generated by that trigger; its unique
//!   `(definition, scheduled_for)` key is the deduplication boundary;
//! - a **run** is one occurrence's execution attempt and its observed outcome;
//! - a definition may bind one **session**, created on the first run through
//!   the existing runtime, so every run of a definition shares the execution
//!   history the runtime already owns.
//!
//! The runtime remains the only execution authority. A run stores the
//! `session_id` and the user-shell `execution_id`; it never mirrors a turn or
//! task lifecycle into a second truth. The dispatcher never runs an agent
//! loop, never calls a model for a shell action, and never starts a second
//! runtime: it connects to the project runtime as an ordinary local client,
//! asks it to execute one `RunUserShell`, observes the terminal event, and
//! disconnects so the existing idle eviction can still retire the daemon.
//!
//! Delivery is at-most-once. An occurrence is claimed atomically under a
//! lease; a dispatcher that dies after delivery leaves an `unknown` run rather
//! than retrying a command whose external effect cannot be proven. This is
//! deliberate: a missed run is visible in history, a silently duplicated one
//! is not.

